------------------------------------------Introduction to Java--------------------------------------

Java is a high-level, object-oriented programming language developed by Sun Microsystems in 1995 (later acquired by Oracle).

It is widely used for:
- Backend development
- Enterprise applications
- Android apps
- Web APIs and services

One of Java’s biggest advantages is **platform independence**.

Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA) – meaning Java code can run on any system that has a JVM.

----------------------Compiled vs Interpreted Languages-------------------------

Programming languages usually run in two ways.

Compiled languages:
- Code is converted to machine code before running.
- Example: C, C++
- Faster execution.

Interpreted languages:
- Code runs line by line using an interpreter.
- Example: Python, JavaScript

Java is actually a mix of both.

Java code is first compiled and then executed by the JVM.



------------------------How Java Works----------------------

1. We write code in a `.java` file  
2. The Java compiler (`javac`) compiles it into bytecode (`.class`)
3. The JVM runs this bytecode

Since JVM exists for many operating systems, the same Java code can run anywhere.

Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA)



------------------JDK vs JRE vs JVM----------------------

These three components make Java work.

1] JVM (Java Virtual Machine)

JVM is the engine that runs Java bytecode.

Responsibilities:
- Executes bytecode
- Memory management
- Garbage collection

2] JRE (Java Runtime Environment)

JRE provides the environment needed to run Java programs.

It contains:
- JVM
- Core Java libraries
- Runtime files


3] JDK (Java Development Kit)

JDK is what developers install to write and build Java programs.

It includes:
- JRE
- Java compiler (`javac`)
- Development tools

4] JIT (Just-In-Time Compiler)

JIT is a part of the JVM that improves performance.

Instead of interpreting bytecode every time, JIT converts frequently used bytecode into native machine code at runtime, making execution faster.

So JVM execution is a combination of:
- interpretation
- JIT compilation

### Relationship

JDK  
└── JRE  
  └── JVM  



--------------------OpenJDK----------------------

OpenJDK (Open Java Development Kit) is the open-source implementation of Java.

Most developers today use OpenJDK instead of Oracle's commercial builds.

Popular OpenJDK distributions include:
- Temurin
- Amazon Corretto
- Microsoft Build of OpenJDK

Functionally they are almost the same for development.


--------------Latest Java Version--------------

Java releases a new version every 6 months.

Some versions are LTS (Long Term Support) versions which are used widely in industry.

Recent important versions:

- Java 17 (LTS)
- Java 21 (LTS)

Currently Java 21 is the latest LTS version used in many modern projects.


--------------Why We Install JDK------------

To develop Java programs we need:

- a compiler to convert `.java` code into bytecode
- the runtime environment to run the program

The JDK provides both, which is why developers install it.
